n terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5ec25fefe799b172b14c5cd0c0fc7d126d8668c9ae1ae3c5f6bcbef52507739f"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}"] ["math" "v(x)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{64-{x}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5ec25fefe799b172b14c5cd0c0fc7d126d8668c9ae1ae3c5f6bcbef52507739f"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "4\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "5\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/3278eca2ae91f59c7c20d91f9190611291924dcc0cd6ffe74456552ba8400335.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3)} \\\\  & ≈-1.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5ec25fefe799b172b14c5cd0c0fc7d126d8668c9ae1ae3c5f6bcbef52507739f"} {"question" "q-122348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-1.7\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-2.6\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/ee516744d915553bf962b498af522cf0006d42c5d0e0e2a003e6f385dd069e4a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8\\sqrt{0+1}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\sqrt[4]{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}(x+1)^{3/4} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{3{C}_{1}}{4}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/4}=3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{4/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{4/3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5ec25fefe799b172b14c5cd0c0fc7d126d8668c9ae1ae3c5f6bcbef52507739f"} {"question" "q-122351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4/3}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=(4t+1)^{3}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=(t+2)^{2/3}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=(t+2)^{4/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4/3}-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=1\\,m$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/f08c9d1e8dce3c0ae7c37719cadc3fc60bf5de3030ce8c44ca7528a98f3abf7e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8{e}^{1-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8{e}^{0}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16{e}^{1-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4{e}^{(1-x)/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{(1-x)/2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=1$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{(x-1)/2}=2t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5ec25fefe799b172b14c5cd0c0fc7d126d8668c9ae1ae3c5f6bcbef52507739f"} {"question" "q-122352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1"] ["math" "x(t)=2{e}^{-2t}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+1"] ["math" "x(t)={e}^{-2t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"} {"question" "q-122352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/state.json" "file_sha256" "3407cde65b40351e41d83dacce10bc7ca1a692bdcccc293aa91aea757e247068"}] "usage" {"q-122342" [] "q-122339" []} "basis" 1901 "retractions" [[17592186317677 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$"] [17592186317691 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186317679 17592186317680 17592186317681 17592186317682 17592186317683 17592186317693 17592186317694 17592186317695 17592186317696 17592186317697] "immutable_fields" [[17592186317678 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317678 :answer-field/id] [17592186317678 :answer-field/key] [17592186317678 :answer-field/type] [17592186317692 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317692 :answer-field/id] [17592186317692 :answer-field/key] [17592186317692 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
